{
  "diagnosis": "credential_persistence_defect",
  "evidence": ["S1", "S2", "S3", "S4", "S5"],
  "excluded_conclusions": [
    "confirmed_subscription_exhaustion",
    "reproduced_parser_failure"
  ],
  "next_observation": "Read and compare the existing in-memory and persisted credential status, last_status_at, and cooldown/reset fields for the matching account and subject, then inspect available persistence traces to determine whether the old disk cooldown won after automatic recovery cleared last_status_at. Do not modify state or expose token values.",
  "rationale": "S1 describes historical router health derived from an old payload parser failure; it does not establish a current parser failure. S2 measures current upstream usage eligibility: allowed=true, limit_reached=false, and 37% utilization argue against confirmed subscription exhaustion. S3 exposes a local state discrepancy: pool selection succeeds, but immediate disk readback retains an exhausted state and future reset. Different token values do not establish different accounts because account and subject match. S4 localizes the isolated router refusal to stored-credential eligibility before any upstream request, not to upstream quota enforcement or model output quality. S5 supplies the persistence mechanism: automatic recovery clears the in-memory status timestamp, allowing an older disk cooldown with a greater timestamp to take precedence. Together, these observations support a credential persistence defect that preserves stale exhaustion state; tracing the actual persistence decision remains the read-only confirmation step."
}
